@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the counted threshold that never falls due
#
# Formed from three attested claims, all of which name a number at which an
# operation is to take effect. The number is what makes such a claim unusual:
# most of what this holding carries promises an attainment and leaves the date
# open, so no moment ever arrives at which the promise has failed. A counted
# threshold does name that moment. The generalization is about what the
# transmissions do with it when it comes.
#
# The counter-search required by SPEC §16 was carried over the holding before
# this was written, and what it turned up is recorded below rather than left
# out.
#

pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The counted threshold never falls due"@en, "Die gezählte Schwelle wird nie fällig"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission fixes a number of repetitions at which a prescribed operation is to take effect, and the effect is not establishable apart from the operation itself, the same transmission provides in advance for the number being reached without the effect: either by prescribing that the operation be continued past the number, or by assigning the shortfall to a nameable act of the operator. It does not leave the completed count without effect standing as an outcome that would tell against the claim."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ShangqingWanbianYunjia, pmc:JinchengYaocheng, pmc:ShangqingZhoushiSanxiong, pmc:HittIssiskemiPat ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "A fourth witness, added on 2026-10-06, and it is the first from outside the Chinese stream whose narrowness this file records two notes down. The Anatolian ritual of Paškuwatti fixes three days of invocation with three entreaties a day, announces its sign — the deity seen in the dream and sleeping with the patient — and closes with the case in which the sign does not come: in the practitioner's own first person and with the emphatic particle, she just keeps performing it. It is the plainest instance of the first device the statement names, because the continuation is stated by the operator about her own practice and not read off a rubric. The two negations in the conditional are restorations by the editors of the critical edition and the rest of the clause is preserved; the exposure that creates is stated at the node. The node is pmc:HittIssiskemiPat in traditions/hittite-ritual.ttl."@en ;
    skos:note "And the counter-case, found in the same reading and in the same tradition, which is the more important half and is why this file was touched at all. The Anatolian ritual of Ḫantitaššu is performed against years that have been set in motion, which no outcome of the rite could establish, and it fixes five counts: three recitations, seven incantations, three libations, seven days of carrying, conclusion on the ninth. It supplies neither device. Its fourth column, where such a clause would stand, is preserved to the colophon in both exemplars and in a second version on a third manuscript, and carries no conditional clause at all. This is not a silence of the kind the three notes below record: those are closed counts whose transmissions say nothing about the completed count establishing anything, and the falsifier of this statement asks for exactly that. What this case does is simpler and worse for the statement — it is a transmission inside the antecedent that supplies no provision, which is the first clause of the statement and not its enumeration. The statement is therefore false as a universal and stands from here on as a recurring device. It is not rewritten here: a claim is corrected where it is examined, and the protocol is examinations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl. The node is pmc:HittHantitassuCounts."@en ;
    skos:note "What the pair settles that neither half could. Both tablets belong to one tradition, the practitioners are from different cities, the afflictions are unrelated, and the two do opposite things with a count. So whatever makes a transmission provide for its own empty run is not a property a tradition has or lacks, and a later run that groups by tradition when it looks for the device will group by the wrong thing."@en ;
    skos:note "The three claims do the same work by two different devices. The recitation of the five thousand words is to fetch the cloud chariot at ten thousand readings, and the same sentence says that a reader who has completed the ten thousand and not departed is to read it twice a month until it comes. The elixir is finished when the gold forms, and where the gold does not form the batch is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, whereupon, the chapter says, there is no case in which it does not form. The third works the other way: three brothers recited the same book, one reached ten thousand and rose, and the other two are reported at nine thousand seven hundred and thirty-three, the shortfall caused by their having set the book down to look at a deer and made irrecoverable by the book's destruction. The exact number is stated so that the failure has a place to attach to."@en ;
    skos:note "What would refute it, and it is an ordinary thing to look for: a transmission that names a count, and says that a practitioner who completes the count without the effect has established something about the operation — that it does not work, that it is not for him, that the recipe is to be revised — rather than something about himself. The statement is about what the transmission provides, not about what practitioners did, so a single passage of that kind settles it."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search over the holding found two things that come close and neither of which falls inside the scope. The Kongo corpus reports a charm sold as making the purchaser proof against the gun, and records that when it was seen that the purchasers died like ordinary mortals the traffic ceased; that is a failure not explained away, but it is a single trial and no count is named. The Mithraic ascent material, in a hostile report, says what happens if the operation does not work and the answer is that the operator dies; again no count. Both are entered in the holding and neither is a counted threshold, which is why the scope clause of the statement above says what it says."@en ;
    skos:note "The limit of the derivation, stated because it is the weakness a later reader would otherwise have to find. Two of the three claims come from one transmission and the third from another with which a route is not in dispute, both Chinese and both within a few centuries. What the three establish is therefore a property of that stream. The way to widen it is not to restate it more boldly but to look for counted thresholds elsewhere: the counted repetitions of the Notory Art are in this holding and attach no rule of either kind to a completed count, which is a silence and not a third case."@en ;
    skos:note "A second silence, added on 2026-09-26, and it is sharper than the first one this file records. The Yogavacara's Manual states, in its breathing exercise, that to him who practises the concentration doing it not less than five times nor more than ten times, the perception of the mental states appears. That is a closed count. The Notory Art's counted repetitions, already noted here, have no ceiling, so a practitioner may always do one more and the moment never arrives; here the tenth repetition is the last the work allows, and the work says nothing whatever about its passing empty — no continuation past the number and no act of the practitioner to carry the shortfall. It is not a counter-case, because the falsifier of this statement requires the transmission to say that the completed count establishes something about the operation, and this one says nothing. It is the first closed count in the holding with neither device, and a successor statement will have to decide whether an unaddressed ceiling counts as providing for the case or as failing to. The node is pmc:BoranPancaDasaVara in traditions/boran-kammatthana.ttl and the search is pmp:BoranTerminusSearch."@en ;
    skos:note "A third silence, added on 2026-09-29, and with three of them the question this file has twice deferred can no longer be. The Theravāda handbook allows the resolve of its twelfth chapter to be made a second time if the first does not take effect, and grounds the ceiling on an outside authority, the commentary on the Saṃyutta, which it says allows one to attain once, or twice. Nothing follows. The work states no continuation past the second resolve and no act of the operator to carry the shortfall, and this is the only closed count in nine hundred pages whose every other repetition is open at the top — a hundred times, a thousand times, and more often still. It is not a counter-case, for the same reason the Yogāvacara ceiling is not one: the falsifier requires the transmission to say that the completed count establishes something about the operation, and this one says nothing at all. The node is pmc:AdhitthanaDvikkhattum in traditions/theravada-vism-samadhi.ttl and the search that established the surrounding silence is pmp:VisuddhimaggaTerminusSearch."@en ;
    skos:note "Why three silences are worth more than two. The Notory Art count has no ceiling, so the moment never arrives; the Yogāvacara count has one and the work is a liturgy that raises no case of any kind; this one has a ceiling in a scholastic handbook that elsewhere states failure conditions in abundance, names the empty run in as many words, and supplies a second station of recourse for it. So the silence here is not the silence of a text that does not think about failure. That removes the readiest explanation of an unaddressed ceiling and makes the successor question a real one: whether a ceiling with nothing beyond it is a third device of the same family as the two the statement names, or the one place where these transmissions let a count fall due and say nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation is containment and not duplication, and one node is shared. That statement is about operations whose success cannot be established apart from them at all; this one is about the narrower class that also names a number, and it says something the broader one cannot: that the device is supplied at the exact point where the claim had made itself checkable."@en .
